---
import Base from '../../../layouts/Base.astro';

const videoId = '1pVoG-3N0EU-xXZDky4ZJpeqXEpvvoXc3';
---

<Base title="Browser SDK error tracking — Xinyi Ye" description="A Datadog proof of concept that fingerprints and groups uncaught Browser SDK errors, then alerts only when a new issue appears.">
  <section class="project-hero shell">
    <a class="back" href="/#work">← Back to the workbench</a>
    <div class="hero-grid">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Analytics systems · observability POC</p>
        <h1>Finding the <em>new</em> error in a sea of repeats.</h1>
      </div>
      <div class="hero-copy">
        <p>Our SDK diagnostics service already captured uncaught errors. The missing piece was identity: every review still required a person to decide whether an error was genuinely new.</p>
        <ul><li>Datadog-native</li><li>No service deployment</li><li>Fingerprint first</li><li>Slack last</li></ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="problem shell">
    <p class="eyebrow">The problem</p>
    <div>
      <h2>Tracking every error is not the same as understanding them.</h2>
      <p>The existing diagnostics service gave us the raw signal, but finding a new SDK failure meant manually reading logs, comparing messages and stack traces, and remembering what we had already seen. This prototype turns that repeated analysis into a deterministic pipeline.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="mechanism">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="section-head">
        <div><p class="eyebrow">The mechanism</p><h2>One event. One stable identity.</h2></div>
        <p>A log is normalized before it becomes an issue. Similar occurrences converge on the same fingerprint; a meaningful change creates a new group.</p>
      </div>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li><span>01</span><div><b>Capture</b><p>The diagnostics service records the uncaught Browser SDK event.</p></div></li>
        <li><span>02</span><div><b>Normalize</b><p>Datadog maps nested values to standard error fields and removes volatile details.</p></div></li>
        <li><span>03</span><div><b>Fingerprint</b><p>Error kind, normalized message, and the first meaningful SDK source frame define identity.</p></div></li>
        <li><span>04</span><div><b>Group</b><p>Matching fingerprints increase one issue's occurrence count instead of creating duplicates.</p></div></li>
        <li><span>05</span><div><b>Notify</b><p>A genuinely new issue group triggers the Slack monitor.</p></div></li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="demo shell">
    <div class="section-head">
      <div><p class="eyebrow">Interactive explainer</p><h2>Follow the fingerprint.</h2></div>
      <div class="actions"><a href="/datadog-error-tracking-demo/" target="_blank">Open full screen ↗</a><a href={`https://drive.google.com/file/d/${videoId}/view?usp=drive_link`} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Watch the demo ↗</a></div>
    </div>
    <div class="demo-frame"><iframe src="/datadog-error-tracking-demo/" title="Interactive Datadog Browser SDK error tracking demo" loading="lazy"></iframe></div>
  </section>

  <section class="video shell">
    <div class="section-head"><div><p class="eyebrow">Two-minute walkthrough</p><h2>See the real path.</h2></div><p>The recording moves from the explainer into real Datadog logs, the grouped Error Tracking issue, and the resulting Slack alert.</p></div>
    <div class="video-frame"><iframe src={`https://drive.google.com/file/d/${videoId}/preview`} title="Datadog Browser SDK Error Tracking demo video" allow="autoplay" allowfullscreen></iframe></div>
  </section>

  <section class="takeaway shell">
    <p class="eyebrow">The takeaway</p>
    <blockquote>Grouping is the product. Notification is the final step.</blockquote>
    <p>Without a stable fingerprint, Slack only makes noise arrive faster. With one, every alert represents a new underlying SDK problem worth investigating.</p>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
